I"<p>Back in 1969, Eb Fetz showed that monkeys could be trained to raise the firing rates of individual neurons.<sup id="fnref:1" role="doc-noteref"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote" rel="footnote">1</a></sup> Decades later, monkeys are trained to do even more complex tasks, such as the so-called “center-out task”, where a monkey moves a mouse cursor controlled by their neural activity alone to one of eight targets.<sup id="fnref:2" role="doc-noteref"><a href="#fn:2" class="footnote" rel="footnote">2</a></sup> With enough training they are able to move the cursor to the target <em>even faster than they could using their hands</em>.</p>

